---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { errorVariants } from '../../data/errors';
import { SRC_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Error surface — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Twelve typed variants naming the exact failure: dimension and metric mismatch, non-finite values, normalization, non-positive operators, non-CPTP channels, freeze-time commutativity, and unfaithful structure.';

const matching = `use deep_causality_quantum::{QuantumError, QuantumErrorEnum};

// The outer newtype carries one constructor per variant, so a call site
// never wraps by hand.
let err = QuantumError::DimensionMismatch("…".into());

// Matching goes through .0, and the freeze failure hands back node
// indices as fields rather than as text to parse.
match err.0 {
    QuantumErrorEnum::CommutatorNonZero { node_j, node_k, .. } => { /* … */ }
    QuantumErrorEnum::NotFaithfullyRepresentable(detail) => { /* … */ }
    _ => {}
}`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/errors/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Reference"
    title="What the crate refuses"
    lede="Every fallible operation returns the same typed error. Reading the variant tells you which invariant broke, without inspecting the matrix that broke it."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/error/quantum_error.rs`}
    sourceLabel="src/error/quantum_error.rs"
  >
    <h2>Shape</h2>

    <p>
      <code>QuantumError</code> is a newtype over
      <code>QuantumErrorEnum</code>. Each variant has a matching constructor
      function on the newtype, so no call site builds the wrapper by hand, and
      matching goes through the tuple field.
    </p>

    <Code code={matching} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      Eleven variants carry a <code>String</code> detail.
      <code>CommutatorNonZero</code> is the exception: it carries the two graph
      node indices as named fields alongside its detail, because a caller that
      learns which pair failed can do something about it, and a caller handed a
      formatted sentence cannot.
    </p>

    <p>
      That distinction survives the trip through the engine.
      <code>freeze_quantum</code> runs inside a hook whose return type is the
      engine's own error, which would flatten the structure into a message. The
      crate stashes the typed error and recovers it after the rollback, so the
      variant reaches the caller intact.
    </p>

    <h2>The twelve</h2>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Variant</th>
            <th scope="col">Payload</th>
            <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
            <th scope="col">Raised in</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {errorVariants.map((v) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code class="ename">{v.name}</code></td>
              <td><code class="epay">{v.payload}</code></td>
              <td class="emean">{v.meaning}</td>
              <td class="ewhere">
                {v.raisedIn.map((m) => <code>{m}</code>)}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Reading the table</h2>

    <p>
      The right-hand column is a grep result rather than a summary of the doc
      comments, so it says where a variant is actually constructed today.
      <code>CalculationError</code> appears in nine modules, which is what a
      catch-all looks like; the narrow variants appear in one or two, which is
      what makes them worth matching on.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three variants are worth knowing before you hit them.
      <code>UnsupportedMetric</code> is what a mixed-signature metric produces
      at the ket bridge, where no single involution is the correct adjoint.
      <code>NonCptpChannel</code> comes from the two CPTP checks, once for a
      negative Choi eigenvalue and once for a trace that misses the identity;
      a Choi operator that is not Hermitian in the first place fails earlier,
      as <code>NonPositiveOperator</code>, since certifying the Hermitian part
      of a non-Hermitian input would be worse than refusing it. And
      <code>NotFaithfullyRepresentable</code> names the three inputs and three
      outputs carrying the C₃ obstruction, so the offending block can be located
      in the graph.
    </p>
  </DocPage>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  th, td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  th { color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody tr:hover { background: var(--bg-1); }
  .ename { font-size: var(--t-mono-sm); color: var(--fg-0); background: none; padding: 0; }
  .epay { font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); color: var(--fg-1); background: none; padding: 0; }
  .emean { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: 36ch; }
  .ewhere { max-width: 22ch; }
  .ewhere code {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
</style>
